Tracing the actual function: _initiateWithdraw is internal override — it is not itself an external entry point, and
  the only way in is through the abstract strategy's redemption entry, which supplies account and sharesHeld from its
  own accounting, not from raw calldata the attacker controls at this frame.

  The decisive fact is the interaction between the guard on line 135 and the call it makes two lines later. The function
  requires PT.isExpired() to be true. _redeemPT branches on exactly that same predicate: when PT.isExpired() is true it
  takes PendlePTLib.redeemExpiredPT(PT, YT, SY, TOKEN_OUT_SY, netPtIn); the PendlePTLib.swapExactPtForToken(...) branch
  — the only branch that touches the Pendle router and the only branch that consumes limitOrderData — is reachable only
  when PT.isExpired() is false. Inside _initiateWithdraw that branch is provably dead: the require has already forced
  the expired path. The claim's exploit primitive is "a reentrant read from the attacker-supplied router/limit-order
  callback," but on this path there is no router call and no limit order at all.

  Reinforcing that: _initiateWithdraw calls _redeemPT(ptAmount, bytes("")) — it hardcodes empty limit-order data. Even
  if the swap branch were somehow reachable, no attacker-supplied bytes are forwarded from here. The only
  attacker-influenced blob in this frame is data, which is forwarded to withdrawRequestManager.initiateWithdraw after
  the redemption and after the approve — i.e. after the branch flip has already been committed, not during a window
  where a stale read could be harvested.

  So the external calls that actually occur are YT.redeemPY / SY.redeem (the Pendle yield token and the SY wrapper —
  protocol-integrated contracts, and the SY implementation is fixed at construction and validated by readTokens()
  against the market) and then the withdraw manager. None of these is an attacker-chosen address; the constructor pins
  MARKET/SY/PT/YT as immutables derived from MARKET.readTokens(), with require(address(PT) == yieldToken). There is no
  attacker-supplied callee in this frame to reenter from.

  The _executeInstantRedemption path is the one that actually decodes params.limitOrderData from user calldata and can
  hit the router, but that is a different function and the finding is filed against _initiateWithdraw:135; and that path
  does not perform the branch flip the claim depends on.

  On the residual pieces: the "never clamps sharesAmount against w.sharesAmount" assertion concerns
  withdrawRequestManager.initiateWithdraw, whose code is not shown here — clamping there is precisely where such a check
  would live, and the tie-break rule says an unshown, assumed-missing guard cannot carry a High. Likewise, "flips the
  caller's own position" is self-affecting by construction: account is threaded from the strategy's own caller context,
  so a user moving their own position from the PT-priced to the escrow-priced valuation is the intended lifecycle
  transition after expiry, not an unauthorized write to a third party's position. And the "already passed a solvency
  check" premise requires a prior in-transaction check whose ordering is in AbstractStakingStrategy, not shown, and not
  demonstrated to be violated.

  Every concrete step the claim names either sits in a provably dead branch, uses hardcoded-empty data, or lives in code
  not presented. Refuted.

  CONSTRAINT|class=C21|a reentrancy or stale-read claim must identify a callback into attacker-controlled code that is
  actually reachable on the traced path, consistent with the branch predicates the guard has already fixed and with the
  literal arguments the calling frame passes, rather than borrowing a callback from a sibling branch that the same guard
  excludes.
